Date: Sun, 24 Jan 93 18:42:13 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: PPL: Communities, laws, etc.

Alot of the PPL discussion is about communities (I don't know the
right term) where interaction between the members is pretty much
voluntary -- you can simply avoid other people who aren't in the
community.  Common examples are various trades and professions, this
mailing list, etc.  So the natural community for contracting law is
the group of people who interact in the given way.

However, certain sectors of one's life are governed by physical
proximity -- physical safety, protection of property from theft,
certain quality of life factors (open space, pollution, visual
attractiveness), etc.  So the natural community for contracting law on
these matters is a compact physical territory.  In sort, a libertarian
version of a town, state, or nation.

Of course, people *are* setting up such systems:  condominiums,
privatized streets, etc.  But for a larger area, you'll have the
interesting phenomenon of signs that say:

	Welcome to Waltham, Mass.

	By entering this town, you are
	consenting to be bound by its laws.

Nothing controversial about that.  In fact, that's the effect of the
present legal regime.  (I've seen T-shirts that say "Welcome to the
Peoples' Republic of Massachusetts -- check your rights at the
border.")

But the intersting point is that contractual communities have *no*
"constitutional" limitations on their laws, and there is no
requirement that their laws be "reasonable", "equitable", etc.  We
could get back to the days when towns would post signs saying "The
speed limit this year is secret.  Violaters will be fined $10."
Driving across country could become interesting, since you'd need an
AAA guidebook to tell you which towns you don't dare drive into.
"Avoid Smurfville -- they confiscate outsiders' cars."

Dale

