Actually, a SIMM is only about 3 inches long and a third of an inch
wide, giving you just the 24 Mbits/sq in you wanted.  However, it is
not sufficient to have storage density ~= texture information
density -- this would imply that to store the texture of a virtual
world, you would need enough DRAMs to cover every surface on the
planet being stored.  Second, you can't get away with texture-mapped
triangles, for reasons which I discussed earlier.  This would suffice
for a limited static walk-through, no more.

