Subject: Re: PHIL: Re: The method of Science, the aim of Religion

> spend 18 hours awake. Even if we assume that two separate new
> events occur throughout this period every six seconds that only
> gives 21,600 events, each of

I think this is a gross underestimate.  An "event" as we see it is
not really *something that the world does* but something we
construct from what the world does.  Only a tiny fraction of the
candidate "events" we could construct ever reach, by virtue of their
interest, the level of consciousness.  And we only really notice a
few of those.  There is an enormous pre-selection for some degree of
"interestingness" happening before you even have the opportunity to
notice an event.  And coincidences that are called examples of
synchronicity are uniformly interesting.  It's not surprising that
the things we notice tend to be interesting to us; nor is it
surprising that they are often "synchronicitous".

> Steve Whitrow

