Date: Mon, 25 Jan 93 10:37:41 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Re: vocabulary q.

Anton Sherwood writes:

> Every time I see the word "emergent", context indicates that it doesn't mean
> what I thought.  Will someone provide the definitive definition?

If there were one, life would be a lot simpler; unfortunately, there
doesn't seem to be.  It's a question I've been interested in for some
time; I can give you some thoughts on what others have said about
the matter:

Webster (sorry Perry) as usual puts it succinctly but not very
helpfully:

 1 emer.gent adj  \i-'m*r-j*nt\
     [ME, fr. L emergent-, emergens, prp. of emergere]
       1 : rising out of or as if out of a fluid
       2  a : arising unexpectedly
          b : calling for prompt action : URGENT
       3 : arising as a natural or logical consequence

Most of my interest in the area regards "emergent computation".
Stephanie Forrest, in the introduction to the special issue of
Physica D (42, 1990), describes it variously as:

  "the behavior of the entire system is in some sense more than
   the sum of its parts"

   Consitituents:
   (i)   A collection of agents, each following explicit instructions
   (ii)  Interactions among the agents (according to the instructions),
	 which formimplicit global patterns at the macroscopic level
	 i.e. epiphenomena
   (iii) A natural interpretation of the epiphenomena as computations

   [presumably the general case is covered by just (i) and (ii)]

   "The important point is that the explicit instructions are at a
   different (and lower) level than the phenomena of interest"

Danny Hillis (Connection Machine inventor) writes in "Intelligence
as Emergent Behavior; or, The Songs of Eden" (Daedalus 117 #1, winter
1988) writes:

  "The rules that govern the forces between water molecules seem
  much simpler than crystals or whirlpools or boiling points, yet
  all of these complex phenomena are somehow consequences of those
  rules.  Such phenomena are called _emergent_phenomena_."

The question had some interest in the late 1920's in philosophical
circles.  For example, Arthur Lovejoy wrote ("The Meanings of
'Emergence' and its Modes", Journal of Philosophical Studies II,
1927):

  "Emergence, then, may be taken loosely to signify any augmentative or
  transmutative event, any process in which there appear evvects that,
  in some one or more of several ways yet to be specified, fail to
  conform to the maxim that 'there cannot be in the consequent anything
  more than, or different in nature from, that which is in the
  antecedent.'"

Making explicit the "several ways yet to be specified" would answer
your question (and mine!) but it hasn't proved possible yet to do
so in a convincing way.

As part of that same conversation, Stephen Pepper ("Emergence",
Journal of Philosophy, 1926), asserted:

  "I assume that a theory of wholesale epiphenomenalism is
  metaphysically unsatisfactory"

... an assumption that is not in vogue today, thank goodness.

Finally (because this is much longer than I originally intended),
Peter Cariani ("Emergence and Artificial Life", Proceedings of AL II),
defines three different types of emergence:

  1) Computational Emergence:  emergent, complex global forms can
  arise from local computational interactions

  2) Thermodynamic emergence:  creation of new thermodynamic structures
  from physical interactions

  3) Emergence relative to a model:  the formation of global structures
  which then act to constrain the local interactions (example: the
  evolution of sensory organs).

At any rate, Cariani asks (but doesn't answer) the right question:
"If we randomly come across a computer simulation and we have no clue
as to its purpose, can we tell if its computations are emergent?"

More generally, if we want to treat things like tornados as emergent
phenomena, by what criteria do we do so?  It is convenient for us
to attach a label to a recognizable phenomena, but what is it that
is inherent in the tornado that makes this true?  This goes back to
the old debate about the reality of "natural kinds" and may also
depend on the particular cognitive aspects of the one labeling the
phenomenon as emergent or non-emergent.

It's a tough problem.  An objective measure of emergence could
revolutionize science.

derek


