   From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>

   I think you're looking at this backwards, Dale.  We don't "get" markets to
   work--we "let" them work.  The market is the default position; it happens
   spontaneously in an atmosphere of non-coercion and respect for property
   rights.  "Organizing things some other way," necessarily means coercion.
   I'm against it.

Yes, but that's not really true.  Unless you're defining "market" to
mean "what you get without a government".

If one is to believe Herodotus, there weren't markets until there was
coined money.  You need certain physical and social infrastructure
before you can get a real "market".

What I mean here by "market" is what an economist would consider a
"market":  a large number of small buyers and sellers, where there is
a price-equilibrium for the product in question.  Libertarian theory
points out the value of markets in producing efficient, fair solutions
to problems.  But the analysis hinges on a "market" existing in the
sense an economist would use.  If something prevents a market from
forming, economic theory (and common observation) shows that the
benefits of a market usually don't happen.

Common things that screw up markets are:
	- domination by one buyer
	- domination by one seller
	- excessive transaction costs
	- poor "transparency" (publication of price information)

Intriguingly, if you look at situations that liberals (modern
definition) complain about, they are often situations where there is
no market, in the true sense of the word, and that the establishment
of a true market would eliminate what the liberals call
"exploitation".

Going back to my first example, before money was invented, it was 
difficult to have a market, because things had to be done by barter.
And that makes it very difficult to have good transparency (what is
the standard of value?) and imposes high transaction costs.  The
invention of money suddenly makes it practical to have a market in
many goods.

