Date: Mon, 25 Jan 1993 15:33:33 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: PPL

 
> In a PPL system, who defines and protects intellectual property?  If
> the PPL over there refuses to respect the patent I got from the PPL
> over here, how do I get my just rights respected?
 
> Dale Worley
 
In my mind, patents are distinctly separate from copyrights.  Patents
involve the use of scientific principles which were not created by the
inventor; they were discovered.  Multiple people frequently work on the same
problem simultaneously, often arriving at identical solutions independently
(and frequently without knowing of the other person's existance, let alone
work).  Should the person who registered his invention first have special
rights to the fruits of his labor; denying an equal right by the others to
benefit from their own work?  
 
As much as I'd like to strech Libertarian theory to cover patents, it can't
be done.  If any PPL enforces patents, it necessarily initiates coercion
(acts like a State) and is therefore not legitimate.  (Unless all the
affected parties have previously agreed to adhere to the first-registered
rule)
 
Copyrights, on the other hand, are different; they involve actual creations,
not discoveries.  I find it implausible that two people would write exactly
the same book independently.  (If that actually happened, however, they
would both have reproduction rights.)  It seems clear to me that this
independently created information can be released on a contractual basis
forbidding unauthorized reproduction and sale.  By buying the book, you
implicitly agree to the terms of the contract (It's reasonable to assume
that they wouldn't have agreed to sell it to you, otherwise.).  This
includes the stipulation that reproduction rights are not bound to the
physical copy; they cannot be abandonded along with that particular book.
If someone finds that abandoned book, he can homestead it, but not homestead
reproduction rights; they weren't included.
 
What happens when someone does reproduce and sell a book without
authorization?  The owner sues for damages.  This does not mean the owner
gets all of those unearned profits; he only gets actual damages.  If the
defendant, for example, can prove that he sold (or gave) the book only to
people who would never have purchased the book otherwise, the plaintiff was
not harmed; he loses the suit.
 
On the other hand, actual damages might far exceed these unearned profits.
For example, releasing the book prematurely might discourage legitimate
sales.  (I'd hate to be on that jury.  This sounds like a pain in the butt
to evaluate.)
 
