Date: Mon, 25 Jan 93 19:17:19 EST
From: sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu (Mark A. Sulkowski)
Subject: PPL: Intellectual Property Rights

	In a previous posting, Perry Metzger said that patents would
not likely exist in a PPL.  I agree that there would not likely be
any absolute monopolies on "patentable" ideas, but there might be
something similar to patents existing in such a society.

	Consider the following:

	Useful, innovative ideas have a market value.  That's why inventors
invent -- at least when they think they will benefit from their effort.
In a PPL, it may be difficult for inventors to internalize these benefits.
In other words, other people may benefit from these ideas without
paying for them.  Inventors need a way to internalize the benefits enough
to make it worth their while to put the effort into inventing.  I believe
it might be possible.

	My recommendation is this.  There may be enough demand from
inventors for companies to be established which record inventions
and restrict the access to and usage of those inventions.  These
inventions may be examined (possibly for a fee), but the examiners
must agree in advance that they may not benefit from the inventions 
they see without paying the inventor the agreed upon fee, or to disclose
the ideas without incurring heavy monetary penalties and/or loosing
the ability to see any record of inventions again.  It could be that
ostracism from invention-record businesses for misbehavior would be
enough to keep free-riding companies in line.

	If someone invents something that has already been invented
and 'patented' by one of these companies, then that inventor would likely
have to go to a different company to have the 'patent' registered there.
So perhaps it would be possible for many different inventors to have
patents to the same thing.  But any one inventor would only make money
if it was 'his' patent that was being used.

	I havn't fleshed this idea out very far, and perhaps it has
been discussed before, but what does eveyone think?  Any obvious
flaws?  Remember one thing though: it isn't necessary for the system
to be perfect, that is, it need not internalize the value of inventing
perfectly.  There may be positive externalities, just not so many that
inventing is not profitable.

